Iʻa Quest is a two-player text-based fishing game that I developed with a three-person team for a Java programming course in Spring 2025. The game takes place over a simulated year, with players competing to catch the greatest total length of fish. Players must also follow fishing regulations based on the species they catch. The regulations include legal sizes, fishing seasons, and permitted catch methods. We presented the completed project at the Student Undergraduate Research Fair (SURF) at Kapiʻolani Community College.

The game was built in Java using object-oriented programming concepts that we had learned throughout the course. Different Hawaiian fish species were represented through a class hierarchy, while an interface defined information on the various regulations. Fish were stored in an 'ArrayList' representing the pond and could grow as the game's months progressed. During each turn, players selected a fishing method, attempted to catch a randomly selected fish, and decided whether to keep or release their catch. Keeping an illegal fish resulted in the player's entire collection of fish being confiscated.

I took on the role of team lead and contributed to several major parts of the game's implementation. I created the initial structure for the main game and worked on integrating the game's core systems. Some of my specific work included the time progression system, the ability for players to return previously caught fish to the pond, and implementing fishing behavior for part of the fish hierarchy.

This project gave me experience working collaboratively on a larger program. I became more comfortable using Git and Github to work with seperate branches and merge contributions from multiple developers. It also gave me experience coordinating a team while continuing to contribute directly to the design and programming of the finished application.
